\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{figures/fig_q2.pdf}
\caption{Q2: factorial contrasts with nominal intervals.}\label{fig:q2}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Stronger attacks and where R3 fails (Q3).} Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope} gives each core rule's attack envelope: the family with the highest training gain, evaluated unchanged. These are finite-search results, not bounds on the families. Against B3 the selected envelope reaches \site{r_q3_f000_f1} on fresh1; against R3 it is \site{r_q3_f111_f1} there, and against C$^*$ \site{r_q3_cs_f1}, from \site{r_q3_cs_f1_fam}. H4 was written in a separate session against the frozen defenders and committed by its hash before the lock; its author's containment note states that it contains D2+H3 exactly as a special case, so it extends the public family rather than offering a disjoint attack concept. Evaluated at the headline market only, its selected search gains \site{r_q3_f111_h4} against R3 and \site{r_q3_f000_h4} against B3. Robustness against D2+H3 is unanswered for every core rule because of search flags, and so is R3's robustness against X7-SC; X5-AG/v2 and H4 carry no search-quality claim. Welfare attacks are the clearest weakness. At the headline market, the selected W0 attacks (no profit constraint) yield gross efficiency \site{r_q3_w0_f000} for B3 and \site{r_q3_w0} for R3, with gains relative to the truthful reference of \site{r_q3_w0g_f000} and \site{r_q3_w0g_f111}; C$^*$ is like R3. Under the prescribed W1 searches (mean gain at least zero), the selected feasible attacks yield efficiency \site{r_q3_w1} for R3, while B3 stays at \site{r_q3_w1_f000} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-welfare}). These finite-search results are not lower bounds on efficiency over a broader policy class. An episode-level mixture, which picks a welfare attack or a profitable attack at the start of each episode, satisfies a mean-gain constraint only when its probability-weighted component gains satisfy that constraint; we examine such mixtures on the existing evaluation records. On the records used to select them, W0 weights of \site{r_ph_w_r3} for R3 and \site{r_ph_w_cs} for C$^*$, with the H4 attack as companion, give estimated normalised gains of \site{r_ph_g_r3} and \site{r_ph_g_cs} and gross efficiencies of \site{r_ph_e_r3} and \site{r_ph_e_cs}; with the public D2+H3 attack as companion the efficiencies are \site{r_ph_e_r3_d} and \site{r_ph_e_cs_d} (Tables~\ref{tab:ph-welfare} and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens}). The registered W1 constraint is a non-negative mean gain on the training seeds. Reconstructed from the search archives, the training mean gains of these mixtures, each at its rule--companion pair's weight in Table~\ref{tab:ph-welfare}, are \site{r_ph_t_r3} and \site{r_ph_t_r3_d} for R3 and \site{r_ph_t_cs} and \site{r_ph_t_cs_d} for C$^*$ (H4 and D2+H3 companions): three meet the training constraint, but C$^*$'s public-family mixture does not. At its evaluation-selected weight of \site{r_ph_wd_cs}, C$^*$'s public-family mixture fails the training constraint. At \site{r_ph_fw_cs}, the largest weight on the same grid at which both the training mean gain and the evaluation bound are non-negative, it has training gain \site{r_ph_ft_cs}, estimated evaluation gain \site{r_ph_fg_cs}, and gross efficiency \site{r_ph_fe_cs}, against \site{r_ph_w1_cs} under the registered W1 search (last row of Table~\ref{tab:ph-welfare}). Both rules therefore show substantial empirical efficiency losses with public-family mixtures alone; H4 is not needed for a drop of this size, and population profitability remains unestablished. The weights are the largest at which a nominal one-sided \const{95} percent normal bound on the mean gain stays non-negative; that bound is not adjusted for selection, the weight and the companion family were chosen on the evaluation seeds, and the bound is close to zero at the selected weights, so this checks the policy class and is not an out-of-sample estimate. The mixture efficiencies stay above \const{0.90}; the analysis does not touch Q4 or its registered predicate. For coalitions of two synchronised members, the selected total gains are \site{r_q3_coal_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c2} against R3 (search flagged); for five, \site{r_q3_coal_f000_c5} and \site{r_q3_coal_f111} (total objective), while the policy selected for the minimum-member objective leaves R3's worst member a mean gain of \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c5min}; the decoy-and-beneficiary family gives \site{r_q3_db_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_db_f111} against R3. These comparisons establish neither coalition stability nor an ordering across every coalition family (Table~\ref{tab:q3-coalitions}). At $\rho = \const{1}$, where R3's terminal reserve collapses, R3 leaves \site{r_rep_f111} under D2+H3; the repair R4 lowers this to \site{r_rep_r4} and C$^*$ with the capped schedule (C$^*$/S2) to \site{r_rep_cs2} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-repair}).
